Short Report: Red Light Green Light Project - Developed in Python OpenCV

In this project, “Red Light, Green Light (RLGL)," I used a Python desktop script (opencv-python) to make a real-time computer vision system. The system uses a state machine to handle live webcam data, find motion, and control how the application works. For this project, we want to learn basic/fundamental computer vision skills and how to use states, timing parameters, and human input to run programs as stated in our learning outcomes.

The algorithm used in this project operates on real-time video frames captured from a webcam. For every frame captured, it goes through preprocessing and motion analysis to identify changes between consecutive frames. The image processing pipeline includes Frame Capture -> Grayscale Conversion -> Gaussian Blur -> Frame Differencing -> Motion Scoring -> Decision Logic -> User Interaction, consecutively. This approach simplifies the idea of image processing and detection, making it practical for real-time execution. While the state machine design allows me to control the program flow by declaring the correct state for the machine to run. These states include IDLE (no significant motion detected), GREEN (allows motion to be detected), WARNING (no motion detected in green state), RED (there should be “NO MOTION or higher than the threshold” detected), and START/RESTART/EXIT state (for user interaction). These state machines make sure that the program/system functions in an established pattern based on the algorithm made.

The parameters that I used in this project were based on the suggested baseline parameters in our PDF file for this project. There is no adjustment made when using those parameters. 

